# CHANGELOG

## 20261011

1.4.0: The method on a redirect is as RFC 9110 has it: a DELETE redirected by a 301 or 302 is now a DELETE, a PUT a PUT, and a HEAD a HEAD.

Every 301, 302, and 303 was followed with a GET, whatever the method. RFC 9110 allows a 301 or 302 to make a POST a GET, for historical reasons, and nothing else: a PUT, a PATCH, a DELETE, an OPTIONS, or a TRACE was turned into a GET, which did something other than was asked, and a HEAD was too, which fetched the body a HEAD is made to avoid. A 303 does make the method a GET, except for a HEAD, which stays one. Those which were made a GET will now be repeated as themselves, a DELETE included, which is what the specification asks but which differs from what was done before, so anyone whose redirected DELETE, PUT, or PATCH was quietly becoming a GET will find it isn't.

1. ~ lib/HTTP/REDIRECT.rb: + HTTP.redirect_method: 307 and 308 keep the method; 303 makes it a GET, a HEAD excepted; 301 and 302 make a POST a GET and keep the rest.
2. ~ HTTP.request: a redirect is made with redirect_method, through request_with_body where the request had a body to repeat and request_without_body otherwise, rather than through the named method. Content-Type is left behind only where the method changes, and a body repeated keeps it through body_headers, as 1.3.11 has a 307 or 308 do, which matters the more now that a PUT or PATCH redirected by a 301 or 302 is repeated with its body rather than made a GET.
3. ~ HTTP.request: a method without a named helper, such as PROPFIND made with request_without_body, is followed. A 307 or 308 was followed with send(request_method), which raised NoMethodError for one.
4. ~ test/HTTP/{delete,head,options,patch,put,trace}_test.rb: a 301 or 302 is expected to be followed with the method itself rather than with GET.
5. ~ test/HTTP/REDIRECT_test.rb: + the method through each of 301, 302, 303, 307, and 308: POST, PUT with its body, DELETE, HEAD, and PROPFIND. The ones which keep a method other than GET or POST fail against 1.3.11, and PROPFIND by 307 and 308 raised. The PUT examples assert its Content-Type as well as its body.
6. ~ README.md: + how the method is kept, under Redirections, with a table of each method through each redirect.
7. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.11/1.4.0/


## 20261011

1.3.11: A repeated body keeps the Content-Type it was sent with.

A 307 or 308 repeats the request with its body, and form data, `HTTP.post(uri, {a: 1})`, was repeated without its Content-Type: `form_data=` sets application/x-www-form-urlencoded upon the request object rather than among the headers given, and the request repeated is a new one, made from those headers. So the server was sent `a=1` and no type to read it by. A body sent with a Content-Type among its headers, JSON or XML, kept it, which is why it went unnoticed. As old as the 307 handling: 1.3.2 does the same.

1. ~ lib/HTTP/REDIRECT.rb: + HTTP.body_headers, which adds the request object's Content-Type to the headers where they carry none of their own.
2. ~ HTTP.request: a 307 or 308 is followed with body_headers, and then redirect_headers, rather than redirect_headers alone.
3. ~ test/HTTP/REDIRECT_test.rb: + form data's Content-Type kept through a 307 and through a 308, both failing against 1.3.10, and a Content-Type given among the headers kept in preference to it, which passes either way, being a guard.
4. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.10/1.3.11/


## 20261011

1.3.10: A block is yielded to once, with the response.

With a block, a followed redirect yielded the final response to it, and then yielded again whatever the block had returned, since that is what the redirected request returned and it was taken for the response: a block which returned an Array was handed that Array. With no_redirect set, the block was yielded the redirect and the redirect was then followed regardless, the branch yielding without returning, so the block was yielded to three times and a request was made which had been asked not to be.

1. ~ HTTP.request: a followed redirect returns what the redirected request returns, which has already been yielded where there is a block, rather than yielding it again.
2. ~ HTTP.request: no_redirect is a condition of following rather than a branch of its own, so that the redirect is yielded or returned as any other response is, and not followed.
3. ~ test/HTTP/REDIRECT_test.rb: + one yield with the final response when a redirect is followed, and one with the redirect when no_redirect is set. Both fail against 1.3.9.
4. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.9/1.3.10/


## 20261011

1.3.9: Only a 301, 302, 303, 307, or 308 with a Location is followed.

Any 3xx was taken for a redirect. A 304 Not Modified carries no Location, so URI#merge was handed nil and raised ArgumentError, as did any 3xx without one. And a 300 Multiple Choices was followed to whatever its Location named, though it is a list of choices for the caller, the Location being only the server's preference among them. These are answers rather than redirects, and are now returned as they are.

1. ~ lib/HTTP/REDIRECT.rb: + HTTP::REDIRECT::CODES (301, 302, 303, 307, 308); + HTTP.redirect?, which is true of a response with one of those codes and a Location.
2. ~ HTTP.request: follows what redirect? is true of, rather than whatever begins with a 3.
3. ~ test/HTTP/REDIRECT_test.rb: + 300 and 304 returned as they are, as is a 302 without a Location. All three fail against 1.3.8: the 300 and the 304 were followed, and the 302 raised.
4. ~ README.md: + which responses are followed, under Redirections.
5. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.8/1.3.9/


## 20261011

1.3.8: Headers are carried through a 301, 302, or 303.

A 301, 302, or 303 was followed with no headers at all, so a User-Agent, an Accept, or a Cookie given to the request was not sent to where it was redirected, whereas a 307 or 308 sent them all. Now every redirect carries them, less the credentials which 1.3.6 keeps to their origin, and less Content-Type where the request is made a GET, there being no body for it to describe.

1. ~ HTTP.redirect_headers: + drop_content_type:, which leaves Content-Type behind.
2. ~ HTTP.request: a 301, 302, or 303 is followed with redirect_headers, dropping Content-Type, rather than with no headers.
3. ~ test/HTTP/REDIRECT_test.rb: + headers carried through each of 301, 302, 303, 307, and 308, and Content-Type left behind when a POST becomes a GET; credentials dropped for another origin by 301, 302, and 303 as well as by 307 and 308. The 301, 302, and 303 examples fail against 1.3.7.
4. ~ README.md: + Redirections, on what is carried through a redirect and what is not.
5. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.7/1.3.8/


## 20261011

1.3.7: The options hash is no longer altered, so no_redirect, retries, and basic authentication survive a call and a redirect.

HTTP.request took no_redirect, username, password, and the retry options out of the caller's hash with delete. A hash passed twice had lost no_redirect by the second call, which then followed the redirect it was asked not to. And the redirect was made with what was left of the hash, so retries and basic authentication were lost at the first redirect, even to the same origin.

1. ~ HTTP.request: works on a copy of the options hash, leaving the caller's as it was.
2. ~ HTTP.request: a redirect is made with the options as given, less what goes no further than the origin.
3. ~ lib/HTTP/REDIRECT.rb: + HTTP.redirect_options, which drops username and password where a redirect goes to another origin, as 1.3.6 does the credential headers.
4. ~ test/HTTP/REDIRECT_test.rb: + the options hash left as it was, no_redirect kept for a second call, retries kept after a redirect, and basic authentication kept for the same origin and dropped for another. All but the last fail against 1.3.6, which dropped it for every origin.
5. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.6/1.3.7/


## 20261011

1.3.6: Authorization and Cookie headers go no further than the origin they were given to.

A 307 or 308 was followed with every header it was given, so an Authorization or Cookie header meant for one host was sent to whichever host the Location named. A 301, 302, or 303 sent no headers on at all, so it did not leak, though only because it lost everything else too. Following curl and browsers, credentials now stay with their origin: the same scheme, host, and port.

1. + lib/HTTP/REDIRECT.rb: HTTP::REDIRECT::CREDENTIAL_HEADERS (Authorization, Cookie); HTTP.same_origin?, and HTTP.redirect_headers, which drops the credential headers, whatever their case, where a redirect goes to another origin.
2. ~ HTTP.request: a 307 or 308 is followed with redirect_headers rather than the headers as given.
3. + test/HTTP/REDIRECT_test.rb: credentials kept for the same origin, and dropped for another host by 307 and by 308, for another scheme, and for another port, with other headers carried on. All but the first fail against 1.3.5.
4. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.5/1.3.6/


## 20261011

1.3.5: Non-ASCII is URL-encoded in full, and without a warning.

A query argument holding a multibyte character was encoded one byte per character, so only the first byte of each went out: 'Älaskår' became '%C3lask%C3r' where it should have been '%C3%84lask%C3%A5r', and a server decoding it got invalid UTF-8. The warning this was first noticed by, "historical binary regexp match /.../n against UTF-8 string", came from the same line and is only a warning, but dropping the /n alone would have left the bytes truncated, which is what kept the warning from seeming to matter.

1. ~ Thoran::String::Urlencode#urlencode: /$1.size/$1.bytesize/, so that each byte of a character is encoded rather than the first only.
2. ~ Thoran::String::Urlencode#urlencode: - /n flag. Matched against a UTF-8 string, a binary regexp warns on current Ruby, and the match is the same without it: a run of non-ASCII characters is still matched whole and then unpacked byte by byte, and a binary string is still matched byte-wise.
3. ~ Thoran/String/Urlencode/urlencode.rb: /0.4.0/0.4.1/ in the file's own header.
4. + test/Thoran/String/Urlencode/urlencode_test.rb: unreserved and reserved characters, a space, two and three byte characters, a binary string, the absence of a warning, and the url_encode alias. The multibyte, warning, and alias examples fail against 1.3.4.
5. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.4/1.3.5/


## 20261011

1.3.4: ~ LICENSE: /2026/2009-2026/

1. ~ LICENSE: the copyright years run from 2009, which is when the README dates the library from. The first commit, 2015, is when its history was imported rather than when it began, so the usual rule of taking the year of the first commit would understate it here.
2. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.3/1.3.4/


## 20260910

1.3.3: ~ lib/HTTP.rb: + require 'HTTP/VERSION'; + test/HTTP/VERSION_test.rb

1. ~ lib/HTTP.rb: + `require 'HTTP/VERSION'`, last among the requires. The file has been present and unreached, so `require 'http.rb'` left HTTP::VERSION undefined and the gemspec was the only thing loading it.
2. + test/HTTP/VERSION_test.rb: that HTTP::VERSION is a string, that it is three numbers separated by dots, and that it matches the newest entry in the changelog. Modelled on moby's, which is where the convention comes from.
3. The test loads the library rather than VERSION.rb, which is the whole of its value: run against v1.3.2 it fails with "uninitialized constant HTTP::VERSION". Nine libraries were in that state and not one of them carried this test, which is how it went unseen.
4. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.2/1.3.3/


## 20260819

1.3.2: ~ http.rb.gemspec: spec.files reordered, the globs first.

1. ~ http.rb.gemspec: spec.files reordered, the two Dir globs first and the loose files alphabetically after them, as moby and measurand have it. The globs are what the gem is and the rest is paperwork, so adding a file to the paperwork now never disturbs them. This gemspec had been pinned first with the globs last, which namo shared and impuri left at its 0.12.1. The same 35 files ship, in the same gem; only the order in the source changes.
2. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.1/1.3.2/


## 20260713

1.3.1: Use Thoran Namespace for URL-encoding extensions.

The String#url_encode and Hash#x_www_form_urlencode extensions reopened the core classes directly. Both now live in named modules under the Thoran:: namespace — Thoran::String::Urlencode and Thoran::Hash::XWwwFormUrlencode — mixed into String and Hash.

1. + Thoran::String::Urlencode#urlencode
2. - String#url_encode
3. + Thoran::Hash::XWwwFormUrlencode#x_www_form_urlencode
4. ~ Hash#x_www_form_urlencode: Requires the Thoran namespaced equivalent.
5. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.3.0/1.3.1/
6. ~ CHANGELOG: + 1.3.1 entry

## 20260610

1.3.0: Rename VERBS to METHODS; VERBS retained as a deprecated alias.

RFC 9110 calls these "methods", not "verbs". HTTP::VERBS becomes HTTP::METHODS and HTTP::RETRY::VERBS becomes HTTP::RETRY::METHODS, with the old names kept as deprecated aliases (via deprecate_constant) so nothing breaks. The call surface — the named methods, the options hash, HTTP.request, request_with_body/request_without_body — is unchanged. The aliases are scheduled for removal in 2.0.0; that removal is the breaking change the major bump is reserved for.

1. /lib/HTTP/verbs.rb/lib/HTTP/METHODS.rb/: the file is now named for the METHODS module it defines, matching RETRY.rb and VERSION.rb.
2. ~ HTTP/METHODS.rb: /VERBS/METHODS/ for the WITHOUT_BODY/WITH_BODY container; + VERBS deprecated alias; /verb/request_method/ in the block variable.
3. ~ HTTP.rb: require 'HTTP/METHODS' in place of require 'HTTP/verbs'.
4. ~ HTTP/RETRY.rb: /VERBS/METHODS/ for the default retry method list; + VERBS deprecated alias. retry_config now accepts the :retry_methods option (preferred) and still honours :retry_verbs as a backwards-compatible alias; its internal config key is /verbs/methods/.
5. ~ HTTP/request.rb: the 307/308 redirect handler now references METHODS::WITH_BODY, and the retry guard reads config[:methods]; /verb/request_method/.
6. ~ test/HTTP/RETRY_test.rb: + retry_methods opt-in and retry_methods-over-retry_verbs precedence cases; the option-leak test now covers both :retry_methods and :retry_verbs.
7. + test/HTTP/METHODS_test.rb
8. ~ README.md: retry_methods documented as the option key, with retry_verbs noted as still accepted; the retry default is shown as HTTP::RETRY::METHODS.
9. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.2.1/1.3.0/
10. ~ CHANGELOG: + 1.3.0 entry

## 20260610

1.2.1: Split the request builders into their own files.

request_with_body and request_without_body, added in 1.2.0, each move into their own file, matching the one-method-per-file layout used throughout lib/. Purely internal reorganisation — the methods, their signatures, and their behaviour are unchanged — so it is a patch, not a minor.

1. + lib/HTTP/request_without_body.rb: HTTP.request_without_body, extracted from verbs.rb; requires net/http and the Hash#x_www_form_urlencode extension.
2. + lib/HTTP/request_with_body.rb: HTTP.request_with_body, extracted from verbs.rb; requires net/http and json.
3. ~ HTTP/verbs.rb: the request builders are extracted; the file now requires the two new files and keeps the VERBS constant and the named-method generation.
4. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.2.0/1.2.1/
5. ~ CHANGELOG: + 1.2.1 entry

## 20260610

1.2.0: Extract request_without_body and request_with_body.

The logic for building request objects — constructing the Net::HTTP request class, encoding bodies, and assembling query strings — lived inside the define_method loops and was unreachable except through the eight named methods. Downstream gems needing a method without a named helper (notably webdav's PROPFIND) had to build raw Net::HTTP request objects and bypass that logic entirely. Those two loop bodies are now module functions, callable directly as HTTP.request_without_body(method, ...) and HTTP.request_with_body(method, ...); the named methods delegate to them and behave identically.

1. ~ HTTP/verbs.rb: + request_without_body() and request_with_body() module functions; the WITHOUT_BODY and WITH_BODY define_method loops now delegate to them.
2. + test/HTTP/request_without_body_test.rb
3. + test/HTTP/request_with_body_test.rb
4. ~ README.md: + Constructing requests for other methods section.
5. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.1.0/1.2.0/
6. ~ CHANGELOG: + 1.2.0 entry

## 20260609

1.1.0: Basic auth via the options hash.

Until now basic authentication was only available through credentials embedded in the URI, which forced callers needing programmatic credentials (notably the webdav gem) to construct their own Net::HTTP request objects and bypass the verb methods entirely. The options hash now accepts username: and password:, extracted the same way no_redirect is, so they never leak through to Net::HTTP. This is additive: URI credentials still work as a fallback, and explicit options take precedence over them.

1. ~ HTTP/request.rb: Extract :username and :password from the options hash; basic_auth now prefers the explicit credentials and falls back to the URI credentials.
2. + test/HTTP/basic_auth_test.rb
3. ~ README.md: + Basic authentication usage section; document the username and password options.
4. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /1.0.0/1.1.0/
5. ~ CHANGELOG: + 1.1.0 entry

## 20260522

1.0.0: API stability declaration.

The verb interface (HTTP.get, HTTP.post, HTTP.put, HTTP.delete, HTTP.head, HTTP.options, HTTP.patch, HTTP.trace), the options hash, the redirect handling, the response predicates, and the retry configuration have been stable in shape for several releases. This release declares that commitment publicly: from here, any breaking change requires a major version bump.

1. ~ HTTP::VERSION: /0.26.0/1.0.0/
2. ~ CHANGELOG: + 1.0.0 entry

